on\": \"The chemical bonds in the hydrogen peroxide snap apart. This usually happens faster if you add a catalyst (a helper substance) or heat. Once the bonds break, the hydrogen and oxygen atoms are free to find new partners.\",\n    \"background\": \"default\",\n    \"atoms\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h1\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": -2.2,\n        \"y\": 1,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o1\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": -1.5,\n        \"y\": 0.5,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o2\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 0,\n        \"y\": 0.5,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h2\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 0.7,\n        \"y\": 1,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h3\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": -2.2,\n        \"y\": -1,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o3\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": -1.5,\n        \"y\": -0.5,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o4\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 0,\n        \"y\": -0.5,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h4\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 0.7,\n        \"y\": -1,\n        \"z\": 0\n      }\n    ],\n    \"bonds\": [\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h1\",\n        \"to\": \"o1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"o2\",\n        \"to\": \"h2\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h3\",\n        \"to\": \"o3\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"o4\",\n        \"to\": \"h4\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"electronTransfers\": [],\n    \"atomOverrides\": {}\n  },\n  {\n    \"number\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Rearrangement\",\n    \"description\": \"As the atoms rearrange, most of them form liquid water.  Each oxygen atom picks up two hydrogen atoms to form a water molecule. The two remaining oxygen atoms are drawn toward each other, beginning to form the O\\u2082 molecule.\",\n    \"background\": \"default\",\n    \"atoms\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h1\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": -2.1,\n        \"y\": 0.8,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o1\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": -1.5,\n        \"y\": 0.3,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h2\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": -0.9,\n        \"y\": 0.8,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h3\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": -2.1,\n        \"y\": -0.8,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o3\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": -1.5,\n        \"y\": -0.3,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h4\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": -0.9,\n        \"y\": -0.8,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o2\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 1.3,\n        \"y\": 0.2,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o4\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 1.3,\n        \"y\": -0.2,\n        \"z\": 0\n      }\n    ],\n    \"bonds\": [\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h1\",\n        \"to\": \"o1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h2\",\n        \"to\": \"o1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h3\",\n        \"to\": \"o3\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h4\",\n        \"to\": \"o3\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"electronTransfers\": [],\n    \"atomOverrides\": {}\n  },\n  {\n    \"number\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Releasing oxygen gas\",\n    \"description\": \"The reaction is complete. Two water molecules (H\\u2082O) are formed, and the two lone oxygen atoms bond together as O\\u2082 gas. Instead of staying in the liquid, this gas bubbles up and fills the empty space in the tube. This high concentration of oxygen is what provides the fuel to relight the glowing splint.\",\n    \"background\": \"default\",\n    \"atoms\": [\n