stable. They force their way into the water molecules, snapping the bonds between the oxygen and hydrogen. This releases a huge amount of heat energy, often melting the sodium.\",\n    \"background\": \"default\",\n    \"atoms\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"na1\",\n        \"element\": \"Na\",\n        \"x\": -0.4,\n        \"y\": 0.6,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"na2\",\n        \"element\": \"Na\",\n        \"x\": -0.4,\n        \"y\": -0.6,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h1\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 0.8,\n        \"y\": 1.2,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o1\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 1.6,\n        \"y\": 0.4,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h2\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 2.4,\n        \"y\": 0.9,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h3\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 0.8,\n        \"y\": -1.2,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o2\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 1.6,\n        \"y\": -0.4,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h4\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 2.4,\n        \"y\": -0.9,\n        \"z\": 0\n      }\n    ],\n    \"bonds\": [\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h1\",\n        \"to\": \"o1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h2\",\n        \"to\": \"o1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h3\",\n        \"to\": \"o2\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h4\",\n        \"to\": \"o2\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"electronTransfers\": [],\n    \"atomOverrides\": {}\n  },\n  {\n    \"number\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Creating sodium hydroxide\",\n    \"description\": \"Each sodium atom donates its single electron to a water molecule. Sodium becomes oxidised. This is a strong base that dissolves instantly into the water. If you added a special indicator liquid, the water would change color to show a base is now present.\",\n    \"background\": \"default\",\n    \"atoms\": [\n      {\n        \"id\": \"na1\",\n        \"element\": \"Na\",\n        \"x\": -0.2,\n        \"y\": 0.4,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"na2\",\n        \"element\": \"Na\",\n        \"x\": -0.2,\n        \"y\": -0.4,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o1\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 1.4,\n        \"y\": 0.3,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h2\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 2.2,\n        \"y\": 0.7,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"o2\",\n        \"element\": \"O\",\n        \"x\": 1.4,\n        \"y\": -0.3,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h4\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 2.2,\n        \"y\": -0.7,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h1\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 3,\n        \"y\": 0.2,\n        \"z\": 0\n      },\n      {\n        \"id\": \"h3\",\n        \"element\": \"H\",\n        \"x\": 3,\n        \"y\": -0.2,\n        \"z\": 0\n      }\n    ],\n    \"bonds\": [\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h2\",\n        \"to\": \"o1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"h4\",\n        \"to\": \"o2\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"electronTransfers\": [\n      {\n        \"from\": \"na1\",\n        \"to\": \"o1\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"from\": \"na2\",\n        \"to\": \"o2\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"atomOverrides\": {\n      \"na1\": {\n        \"radiusScale\": 0.7,\n        \"dimmed\": true\n      },\n      \"na2\": {\n        \"radiusScale\": 0.7,\n        \"dimmed\": true\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"number\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Hydrogen gas and energy\",\n    \"description\": \"The reaction is complete. Two sodium hydroxide (NaOH) units are formed in solution. The two freed hydrogen atoms bond together as a gas, producing the energetic fizzing and popping you see